22. TESTNG REAL-TIME 🔴
409. 500 tests need parallel execution. How?
Interview answer

“If I have around 500 tests, I would not execute them sequentially because execution time would be very high. I would use TestNG parallel execution with a controlled thread count.

I would categorize tests into groups such as smoke and regression and execute them in parallel. I would also make sure the framework is thread-safe, especially the WebDriver management and test data.

I normally use parallel="tests", parallel="classes" or parallel="methods" depending on the framework design, and configure an appropriate thread-count.”

testng.xml
<suite name="Regression Suite" parallel="tests" thread-count="10">

    <test name="Chrome Tests">
        <classes>
            <class name="tests.LoginTest"/>
            <class name="tests.OrderTest"/>
        </classes>
    </test>

    <test name="Firefox Tests">
        <classes>
            <class name="tests.PaymentTest"/>
        </classes>
    </test>

</suite>
Important point

Don't simply say:

“I will use 500 threads.”

That's bad practice.

You choose the thread count based on:

Machine resources
Grid/cloud capacity
Browser instances
Application/environment capacity
Test dependencies
CI agent capacity
410. Two tests use same browser. Problem?
Interview answer

“Yes, it can create problems if both tests share the same WebDriver instance. One test may navigate to another page, close the browser, modify cookies or change the application state while the other test is still executing.

For parallel execution, I create an independent WebDriver instance per test/thread rather than using one static shared driver.”

Bad approach
public static WebDriver driver;

Multiple parallel tests can interfere with each other.

Better approach — ThreadLocal
private static ThreadLocal<WebDriver> driver =
        new ThreadLocal<>();

public static void setDriver(WebDriver webDriver) {
    driver.set(webDriver);
}

public static WebDriver getDriver() {
    return driver.get();
}

public static void unload() {
    driver.remove();
}

Now each thread gets its own driver.

Strong interview statement

“I avoid sharing mutable WebDriver state between parallel tests. I use ThreadLocal or a driver factory that creates one driver per execution thread.”

411. Tests fail because of shared data.
Example

Test A:

Create customer → ABC

Test B:

Update customer → ABC

When both run simultaneously, they may interfere.

Interview answer

“If tests are failing because of shared test data, I first identify whether the data is static, dynamically generated or environment-dependent. For parallel execution, I prefer creating independent test data for each test using unique identifiers. If required, I can use DataProviders, database setup scripts, APIs or test-data factories to generate isolated data.”

Example
String email =
    "test_" + System.currentTimeMillis() + "@gmail.com";

Or:

String username =
    "user_" + UUID.randomUUID();
Best practice
Test
 ↓
Generate unique data
 ↓
Execute
 ↓
Validate
 ↓
Cleanup
Senior-level statement

“I don't solve shared-data problems by simply disabling parallel execution. I first try to make the test data independent.”

412. Tests depend on one another.

Example:

Create Account
      ↓
Login
      ↓
Place Order
      ↓
Payment
Interview answer

“Ideally, automation tests should be independent because independent tests are easier to execute in parallel and easier to debug. If there is a genuine business dependency, I can use TestNG dependencies, but I avoid creating unnecessary test-to-test dependencies.”

TestNG
@Test
public void createAccount() {
    // create account
}

@Test(dependsOnMethods = "createAccount")
public void login() {
    // login
}

@Test(dependsOnMethods = "login")
public void placeOrder() {
    // order
}
Important

If createAccount() fails:

createAccount ❌
      ↓
login SKIPPED
      ↓
placeOrder SKIPPED
Better framework approach

Whenever possible:

Test A → independent test data
Test B → independent test data
Test C → independent test data

rather than:

A → B → C → D
413. One test fails and you don't want remaining tests.

This question can mean dependent tests or stop execution after failure.

Option 1 — Dependency
@Test
public void login() {
}

@Test(dependsOnMethods = "login")
public void checkout() {
}

If login fails, checkout doesn't execute.

Option 2 — Stop the entire suite

This requires custom listener/runner logic rather than simply depending every test on one test.

Interview answer

“If only specific tests should stop after a failure, I use TestNG dependencies. If the requirement is to terminate the complete execution after the first failure, I implement that at the suite/listener or CI execution level depending on the requirement.”

Don't say:

“I always stop the complete suite when one test fails.”

Because in regression execution, normally we want remaining independent tests to continue.

414. Only smoke tests should run.

Use groups.

Test
@Test(groups = "smoke")
public void loginTest() {
}
@Test(groups = "regression")
public void paymentTest() {
}
testng.xml
<groups>
    <run>
        <include name="smoke"/>
    </run>
</groups>

Now only:

smoke
smoke
smoke

tests execute.

Interview answer

“I use TestNG groups to categorize tests such as smoke, regression, sanity, API and UI, and then include the required group from testng.xml or from the Maven/CI command.”

415. Only regression tests should run.

Same concept.

@Test(groups = "regression")
public void paymentTest() {
}
@Test(groups = "regression")
public void orderTest() {
}
XML
<groups>
    <run>
        <include name="regression"/>
    </run>
</groups>
416. Run tests based on groups.

You can assign multiple groups.

@Test(groups = {"smoke", "regression"})
public void loginTest() {
}

Another:

@Test(groups = {"regression", "payment"})
public void paymentTest() {
}

Then:

<groups>
    <run>
        <include name="smoke"/>
    </run>
</groups>

or:

<groups>
    <run>
        <include name="regression"/>
    </run>
</groups>
Real framework grouping

I commonly use something like:

smoke
sanity
regression
api
ui
critical
Strong answer

“Groups give us flexibility because the same test can belong to multiple execution categories.”

417. Retry only failed tests.

TestNG supports retry using IRetryAnalyzer.

Retry class
public class RetryAnalyzer
        implements IRetryAnalyzer {

    private int count = 0;
    private int maxRetry = 2;

    @Override
    public boolean retry(ITestResult result) {

        if (count < maxRetry) {
            count++;
            return true;
        }

        return false;
    }
}

Then:

@Test(retryAnalyzer = RetryAnalyzer.class)
public void loginTest() {

}
Result
First execution → FAIL
Retry 1         → FAIL
Retry 2         → PASS
Real-time answer

“I use retry selectively for known transient failures such as environment instability or temporary network issues. I don't use retry to hide genuine application defects.”

🔥 This is an important senior-level point.

418. Capture screenshot automatically.

I normally use a TestNG listener.

For example:

public class TestListener
        implements ITestListener {

    @Override
    public void onTestFailure(ITestResult result) {

        // capture screenshot
    }
}

Screenshot logic:

TakesScreenshot ts =
        (TakesScreenshot) DriverFactory.getDriver();

File source =
        ts.getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE);

Then save it with the test name.

Why listener?

Without listener:

@Test
public void loginTest() {

    try {
        // test
    } catch (Exception e) {

        screenshot();

        throw e;
    }
}

You would have to repeat screenshot logic in every test.

With listener:

Test fails
    ↓
ITestListener
    ↓
onTestFailure()
    ↓
Screenshot
    ↓
Log
    ↓
Report
Strong answer

“I prefer listener-based screenshot capture because it is centralized and automatically applies to all tests without duplicating code.”

