“I don't manually send reports from individual tests. Reporting is handled centrally through the framework and CI pipeline.”

420. Run DataProvider in parallel.

This is a very important TestNG question.

Normal DataProvider
@DataProvider(name = "loginData")
public Object[][] loginData() {

    return new Object[][] {
        {"user1", "pass1"},
        {"user2", "pass2"},
        {"user3", "pass3"}
    };
}
Parallel DataProvider
@DataProvider(
    name = "loginData",
    parallel = true
)
public Object[][] loginData() {

    return new Object[][] {
        {"user1", "pass1"},
        {"user2", "pass2"},
        {"user3", "pass3"}
    };
}

Then:

@Test(dataProvider = "loginData")
public void loginTest(String username,
                      String password) {

    System.out.println(username);
}

Execution can become:

Thread 1 → user1
Thread 2 → user2
Thread 3 → user3
Important

You still need a proper:

Thread-safe WebDriver
+
Independent test data
+
Appropriate thread count

Otherwise parallel DataProvider can create flaky tests.

421. Run tests across Chrome/Firefox/Edge.

This is basically cross-browser testing + parallel execution.

I would parameterize the browser.

testng.xml
<suite name="Cross Browser"
       parallel="tests"
       thread-count="3">

    <test name="Chrome">
        <parameter name="browser" value="chrome"/>

        <classes>
            <class name="tests.LoginTest"/>
        </classes>
    </test>

    <test name="Firefox">
        <parameter name="browser" value="firefox"/>

        <classes>
            <class name="tests.LoginTest"/>
        </classes>
    </test>

    <test name="Edge">
        <parameter name="browser" value="edge"/>

        <classes>
            <class name="tests.LoginTest"/>
        </classes>
    </test>

</suite>
TestNG
@Parameters("browser")
@BeforeMethod
public void setup(String browser) {

    if (browser.equalsIgnoreCase("chrome")) {
        driver = new ChromeDriver();
    }
    else if (browser.equalsIgnoreCase("firefox")) {
        driver = new FirefoxDriver();
    }
    else if (browser.equalsIgnoreCase("edge")) {
        driver = new EdgeDriver();
    }
}

Execution:

             LoginTest
                 |
       ---------------------
       |         |         |
    Chrome    Firefox     Edge
    Thread1   Thread2    Thread3
Real company approach

For a larger framework, I wouldn't hard-code browser creation inside the test.

I'd use:

TestNG
   ↓
Browser parameter
   ↓
DriverFactory
   ↓
Chrome / Firefox / Edge
   ↓
ThreadLocal<WebDriver>

And for large-scale execution:

TestNG
   ↓
Selenium Grid / Cloud
   ↓
Chrome
Firefox
Edge
🔥 VERY IMPORTANT: How to answer these as a 5-year SDET

If the interviewer asks:

"How do you execute 500 tests in parallel?"

Don't give only:

"I use parallel=true."

Give this:

“For a large regression suite, I categorize tests using TestNG groups and execute them in parallel with a controlled thread count. I use ThreadLocal WebDriver so each parallel thread gets its own browser instance. I also ensure test data is isolated because shared data can cause race conditions. For cross-browser execution, I parameterize the browser and use a DriverFactory. In CI, depending on the scale, I can distribute the execution across Selenium Grid or a cloud execution platform. After execution, listeners handle screenshots, logging and reporting.”

That answer sounds much more like a real SDET.

